  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1227 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nInto Monday, the aforementioned OR low will be ejecting into the\nnorthern Rockies while the upper high nudges slightly eastward. \nThis will drag some drier air closer to the Four Corners, but \noverall most of the moisture in our forecast area will stay \nintact, keeping storms firing, especially from the Gila \nnortheastward to the Sangre de Cristos. The monsoon plume of \nmoisture will not alter all that much into Tuesday with just a \nminor west-east elongation and a strengthening of the upper high \nover the deep southern states. However, if the high continues to \nelongate like this into Wednesday, it will likely disrupt the \nplume some, pushing it farther west into AZ. There are indications\nthat a faint frontal boundary would sag into eastern CO \nWednesday, but there is not a clear cut consensus that this would \nseep into northeastern NM. \n\nThe high would then start to center closer to AZ and NM into \nThursday and Friday in a slightly more weakened state (500 mb \nheights of 590-594 decameters). With PWATs still staying in a 0.8 \nto 1.1 inch range for most zones, scattered coverage of storms \nwould still ensue. Overall, temperatures look to keep a similar \ntrend Wednesday through Friday of next week with near normal \nreadings being most common in the western zones and anomalously \nhigh readings (by 6 to 12 degrees) staying in the eastern half of\nthe state.",
